WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.734
Your hello-devops image is mostly other people's work.

2
00:00:06.734 --> 00:00:09.253
Your own code is one file, app.py.

3
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:19.263
The rest is 87 Debian packages from the base image, Python itself, and about 10 Python packages, such as Flask, gunicorn and redis.

4
00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:25.563
Everything your code passes through on its way to production is your software supply chain.

5
00:00:25.563 --> 00:00:27.891
Follow it from source to a running Pod.

6
00:00:29.867 --> 00:00:34.065
A supply chain attack goes after one of these links instead of your code.

7
00:00:34.065 --> 00:00:38.264
It works because you trust the pieces, and run them without reading them.

8
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.193
It starts with your own code.

9
00:00:40.193 --> 00:00:43.471
A leaked token caught before git push costs a minute.

10
00:00:43.471 --> 00:00:46.996
Found in production, it costs a weekend of rotating keys.

11
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:48.943
Next, the libraries.

12
00:00:48.943 --> 00:01:01.611
In 2024, a contributor who had earned the maintainer's trust hid a backdoor in xz, a small compression library, in versions 5.6.0 and 5.6.1.

13
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.593
It sat in the release tarballs, not in the git repository, where reviewers look.

14
00:01:06.593 --> 00:01:09.109
So you need to know exactly what you ship.

15
00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:10.429
Then the build.

16
00:01:10.429 --> 00:01:18.877
In March 2025, attackers moved the version tags of the tj-actions/changed-files action to a malicious commit.

17
00:01:18.877 --> 00:01:24.689
Workflows that used those tags printed their CI secrets into the build log.

18
00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:34.196
In March 2026, the scanner Trivy was hit the same way: 76 of the 77 trivy-action tags were force-pushed.

19
00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:35.544
A tag can be moved.

20
00:01:35.544 --> 00:01:39.748
A commit SHA cannot, because it is a hash of the commit's content.

21
00:01:39.748 --> 00:01:43.697
So the workflow pins every third-party action to its full SHA.

22
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:49.943
At the registry, someone can push hello-devops:1.2.0 again, with new content.

23
00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:51.757
A digest cannot change.

24
00:01:51.757 --> 00:01:54.361
So the workflow signs the digest.

25
00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:57.693
The last link is the cluster, where the Pod runs.

26
00:01:57.693 --> 00:02:01.110
Signatures protect nothing until someone checks them.

27
00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.044
So you verify, before you run.

28
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.241
Now the checks.

29
00:02:06.241 --> 00:02:15.475
An SBOM, a software bill of materials, is the ingredient list of the image: every package and version, in a format machines can read.

30
00:02:15.667 --> 00:02:21.433
The tool syft reads an image and writes one, in SPDX or CycloneDX format.

31
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:28.240
A scanner such as Trivy or grype matches that list against public databases of known holes.

32
00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.085
Each hole has a CVE ID, and a severity.

33
00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:36.820
The release workflow fails on HIGH and CRITICAL findings that already have a fixed version.

34
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.017
Keep every SBOM.

35
00:02:38.017 --> 00:02:44.053
On the morning the next xz is announced, you search the files, and have your answer in minutes.

36
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.374
Next, sign the image.

37
00:02:46.374 --> 00:02:51.609
A signature answers two questions: who made it, and has anyone changed it since?

38
00:02:51.609 --> 00:02:55.665
cosign, from the Sigstore project, signs without a stored key.

39
00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:08.116
The release job asks GitHub for an OIDC token: a short signed note that says, I am release.yml in this repository, running for tag v1.2.0.

40
00:03:08.267 --> 00:03:11.850
cosign makes a fresh key pair that lives only in memory.

41
00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:15.882
Fulcio checks the token, and issues a certificate for that key.

42
00:03:15.882 --> 00:03:20.361
It is valid for 10 minutes, with the workflow identity written inside.

43
00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:27.793
cosign signs the image digest, never a tag, and stores the signature in the registry, next to the image.

44
00:03:27.933 --> 00:03:32.946
The signing event also goes into Rekor, a public log that only grows.

45
00:03:32.946 --> 00:03:36.288
Nobody can quietly remove or rewrite an entry.

46
00:03:37.067 --> 00:03:38.861
A signature says who signed.

47
00:03:38.861 --> 00:03:46.488
Provenance says how the image was built: which repository, which commit, which workflow file, and which kind of runner.

48
00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:53.755
The workflow calls actions/attest twice: once for the SLSA provenance, and once to attach the SBOM.

49
00:03:53.755 --> 00:03:57.048
Both are signed, and bound to the same digest.

50
00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.753
GitHub's docs warn that attestations alone give no security benefit.

51
00:04:02.753 --> 00:04:05.765
Someone has to check them, before the deploy.

52
00:04:05.933 --> 00:04:12.852
cosign verify names the identity you expect: your workflow file and tag, and GitHub's token issuer.

53
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:21.540
It checks that the signature matches the digest, that Fulcio issued the certificate, and that the identity inside is the one you named.

54
00:04:21.733 --> 00:04:24.364
An image signed by anyone else fails.

55
00:04:24.533 --> 00:04:29.533
gh attestation verify checks the provenance and the SBOM the same way.

56
00:04:29.533 --> 00:04:33.891
When all of it passes, deploy that exact digest, not the tag.

57
00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:35.989
Each link gets its own check.

58
00:04:35.989 --> 00:04:39.371
And the last check is yours: verify before you run.
